><span>People love mayors</span></h2><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geor=
gia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Zohran Mamdani is not just popu=
lar; he=E2=80=99s more popular than he was when elected.</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>W=
e live in anti-incumbent times. In 2024, every incumbent across the world=
=E2=80=99s major developed economies lost vote share in national elections.=
 And yet Eduardo Paes, the former center-right mayor of Rio de Janeiro, won=
 a fourth term in office that year with more than 60 percent of the vote. A=
ndre Dickens, the center-left mayor of Atlanta, was re-elected the next yea=
r with 85 (!) percent of the vote.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/2=
5px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Not every local leader =
is popular, of course. There are corrupt mayors, mayors who disappoint, may=
ors who are voted out like any other politician. Still, it=E2=80=99s striki=
ng how many of them, on both sides of the political divide, seem to hold on=
 to voters=E2=80=99 trust even at a time when so many national politicians =
lose it so quickly.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,ser=
if;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Struggling leaders have at times even =
sought out mayors in the hope that some of their magic rubs off: At the hei=
ght of the yellow vest protests, President Emmanuel Macron held televised t=
own hall meetings with mayors across France and posed with some 600 of them=
 to bolster his approval rating. It worked, at least for a while.</span></p=
