margin:0 0 15px"><span>This summer, Andy Burnham became prime minister of B=
ritain. Burnham was a popular mayor of Greater Manchester, who had won thre=
e consecutive terms. He=E2=80=99s also taking on a particularly challenging=
 office. Britain has gone through six prime ministers in the last 10 years.=
</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margi=
n:0 0 15px"><span>There </span><span style=3D"font-style:italic">are</span>=
<span> lessons national leaders can learn from mayors and Burnham, in parti=
cular, seems keen to apply them. Can they help him survive=3F</span></p><p =
style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><s=
pan style=3D"font-weight:700">A human scale</span></p><p style=3D"font:norm=
al 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It=E2=80=99s p=
erhaps no coincidence that democracy was born in Athens, a city-state. The =
most obvious advantage mayors have over national leaders is that cities are=
 small. Mayors know their voters, and their voters know them.</span></p><p =
style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><s=
pan>I spoke to Alexander Heffner, a journalist who recently met the mayors =
of 10 cities for an ongoing PBS documentary series called =E2=80=9C</span><=
a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/a/IgqhKlSXWXp4kAHezjcDnw~~/AAAAARA~/PV-r=
Evjd_uataCBGHzMtN9I_FU8aYws3iH2v-skosogzJ0ZymAUDDAbx4j3iInHAxjYb2kKPj8Wi4zs=
